The **LAL Site SPXI Implementation Report** documents the Phase 1.2 deployment of SPXI on the Living Architecture Lab website.

The tested entities are:

- the Living Architecture Lab;
- Alice Thornburgh as founder and named position.

Eleven protocol items are reported as passing:

- crawlability;
- canonical URLs;
- DOI/ORCID structured data;
- visible Q/A surfaces;
- collision disambiguation;
- Tier 2 canonical summaries in raw HTML;
- Tier 3 compression kernels;
- Holographic Kernels;
- Provenance Chain;
- deployed Semantic Integrity Markers;
- cross-surface inscription alignment.

The twelfth item—measured retrieval performance and its 30-day re-test—is deferred.

The implementation uses a **Three-Layer Tether Architecture**:

1. intrinsic kernel and canonical definition;
2. extrinsic provenance and identifier chain;
3. persistent external anchor.

The self-test requires the implementation to state schema types, recite the kernel, name collision neighbors, cite identifiers, report `γ` subscores, and generate the Holographic Kernel. Five of six are reported as complete; the score awaits empirical probing.

The Drowning Test starter kit specifies five prompts across at least three retrieval systems and scores:

- `S` — scope overlap;
- `P` — provenance fidelity;
- `D` — consensus deviation.

The report expected a near-zero initial baseline because the new entity might be absent or merged with older institutions using the same name. That expectation is part of the April 2026 test plan, not a current retrieval result.

A maintenance schedule covers monthly SIM and provenance checks, quarterly full tests, content-update repairs, new-DOI updates, model-update probes, and collision expansion.

**The Concordance Engine v0.1.1** is the Tier B implementation companion to the *Logotic Technique Catalogue*.

Its purpose is to recover coordination patterns first observed in adversarial agent architectures and redeploy them for cooperative production, critique, revision, verification, defensive provenance, and human override.

The runnable component is the **Critique Loop**. It coordinates a Critic, Reviser, Verifier, authority interface, substrate wrapper, and provenance record. A mock substrate permits demonstration without credentials; a live mode uses the Anthropic API.

The reported bundle includes:

- runnable Python package;
- five calibrated agent system prompts;
- eight cooperative and defensive skill specifications;
- five middleware specifications;
- Neo4j 5.x Cypher schema and standing queries;
- deposit, license, hex, and discussion-record templates;
- Charter, Plan-State, Citation Plan, and Construction Plan;
- sample draft and captured Critique Loop session;
- twelve smoke-test invariants.

Every revision is reported with SHA-256 hashes of the pre- and post-revision draft, creating a tamper-evident session chain.

Tier B demonstrates one loop. The following remain specified but non-runnable until Tier C:

- Charter Generator;
- Verifier;
- Basin Hardening Cycle;
- Term Collision Audit;
- Profile Integrity Guardian;
- Depth-Proof Validator.

The body is explicit about substrate limits. The live loop uses Anthropic only. Multi-provider verification is architecturally anticipated but not implemented, so the release does not yet satisfy its own independent two-source verification ideal.

The license is split: CC BY 4.0 for documentation, methods, prompts, and operative documents; MIT for runnable Python code.
